---
`site/mnt/<name>` is a symlink to a folder somewhere else on disk. Those are
the only paths allowed to leave `site/`, and the only ones that get a directory
listing — everything else on this site 404s rather than showing you a folder.

    ln -s ~/repos/buks site/mnt/buks

That's it. [[colophon|The mount]] then appears at `/mnt/buks`, browsable, with
sizes, and files stream straight from disk — a 50MB PDF never touches the
server's memory.

## containment

The textual `..` reject stops the obvious attack. Then `realpath()` of the
resolved target must still sit under `realpath()` of the mount root, which also
catches a symlink *inside* the mounted tree pointing at `/etc`. Dotfiles are
never listed or served.

## before you point this at the internet

A mount publishes everything under it, recursively, to anyone who can reach the
server. Right now that is localhost only. Check what is actually in the folder
before this moves to a VPS — and note that mounts are in `.gitignore`, because
a symlink to an absolute path means nothing in a clone and leaks your directory
layout.
